<svelte:head><title>Voynich@home · Volunteer computing for Voynich research</title><meta name="description" content="Help test focused search methods against known messages before the project applies them to the Voynich manuscript."/></svelte:head>
<div class="page">
  <section class="hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Volunteer computing · development preview</p><h1>Help test search methods for the Voynich manuscript.</h1><p class="lede">Voynich@home is building a volunteer computing platform for focused research tasks with fixed work limits. Before any manuscript campaign opens, each search method must first recover known messages.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/research/development">Review the development results →</a><a class="text-link" href="/contribute">See how browser participation works →</a></div><p class="small muted">No public campaign is accepting work. The frozen fresh-case evaluation is still running, and the project has not published its results.</p></div>
  <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-label="Research sequence"><div class="figure-top"><span>How the research works</span><span>01–03</span></div><div class="figure-step"><span class="number">01</span><div><h3>Start with a known message.</h3><p>Encode a known text with a defined method, then measure whether the search recovers it.</p></div></div><div class="figure-step"><span class="number">02</span><div><h3>Use the same test for controls.</h3><p>Search shuffled and generated texts under the same limits, and keep every failure.</p></div></div><div class="figure-step"><span class="number">03</span><div><h3>Publish enough to reproduce it.</h3><p>Release the rules, unchanged output, and reproduction files for review.</p></div></div><figcaption class="figure-caption">Successful and unsuccessful searches follow the same tests.</figcaption></figure></section>
  <div class="strip"><div><span class="badge">Current study · fresh known messages</span></div><p>The first development panel is complete. The project froze its settings and is testing them on new message-and-key cases. Manuscript searches remain exploratory until recovery tests establish a useful range. <a href="/experiments">Review the research record →</a></p></div>
  <section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Ways to contribute</h2><p class="small muted">Volunteer computing, research review, and open-source work.</p></div><div class="cards"><article class="card"><div class="card-number">01 / VOLUNTEERS</div><h3>Lend your browser time.</h3><p>Once a campaign opens, you choose when to start, how much processing time to use, and when to stop. The server awards credit after it reproduces your result.</p><a href="/contribute">See how browser participation works →</a></article><article class="card"><div class="card-number">02 / RESEARCHERS</div><h3>Propose a focused test.</h3><p>Review the assumptions and sources, then propose an experiment with fixed comparisons and stopping conditions.</p><a href="/methods">Read the research methods →</a></article><article class="card"><div class="card-number">03 / CONTRIBUTORS</div><h3>Build and reproduce.</h3><p>Improve the Rust search code, browser experience, language resources, or operating checks using the published task formats.</p><a href="/downloads">Build the project locally →</a></article></div></section>
  <section class="prose" style="margin-top:75px"><p class="eyebrow">The manuscript</p><h2>An open historical question</h2><p class="lede">Yale’s Beinecke Library holds the illustrated Voynich manuscript. Its writing, illustrations, and structure offer clues, but scholars have not broadly accepted any proposed reading.</p><p>This project tests precise claims and publishes the evidence needed to inspect them. More computation helps only after a bounded search method earns further testing.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://beinecke.library.yale.edu/collections/highlights/voynich-manuscript">Read about the manuscript at Yale’s Beinecke Library ↗</a></section>
